WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.669
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Lillie Geismar, who's an associate professor

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:08.820
of history at Claremont McKenna College and
also author of the book Left Behind the Democrats

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.360
Failed Attempt to Solve Inequality. Really
great having you on today. I appreciate your

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.641
time.
Speaker 3: Oh, thank you so much for the invitation.

00:00:17.641 --> 00:00:19.950
Speaker 1: So, I mean, I think an interesting
place to start or a logical place to start

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:26.080
would be most of my audience, I'm sure, would
agree that at least the rhetoric and the promises

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:33.120
of the Democratic Party relative to the Republican
Party would seem more likely to reduce inequality

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.350
and to sort of level the economic disparities
that we see in the United States. And yet,

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:44.680
to a great degree, it really hasn't happened.
And we could talk about why that's the case.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:50.609
We could say, well, it's it's despite every
good intention, it's simply Republican obstructionism

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:55.309
and sort of shift the blame elsewhere and
and take that approach. But you take a different

00:00:55.309 --> 00:01:00.239
approach in the book. Can you talk a little
bit about the way you see what you call that,

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:04.440
this failed attempt to deal with inequality?
Speaker 3: Absolutely. And I think that one

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.700
of the issues which you've identified and
is not and this is not to say that the Republican

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.950
I think the Republican Party is blameless
in efforts to address inequality. But I think

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.460
so often there's an effort to just sort of
say this. It's all about putting putting blame

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.750
solely on the Republican Party for its policies.
And one of the things I really wanted to understand

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:31.740
is that why inequity has increased so dramatically
in the United States since the since the 1970s,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:37.979
